adjudication.rationale:
In `store.js.es6` `_hydrateEmbedded`, the new plural branch handles keys ending in `_ids` by always mapping the id list to `_lookupSubType(...)` results and then unconditionally deleting the original `*_ids` key:

```js
const hydrated = obj[k].map(id => self._lookupSubType(...));
obj[self.pluralize(subType)] = hydrated || [];
delete obj[k];
```

Compared to the existing singular `_id` behavior, this is a real correctness regression: the singular path only deletes `*_id` if a truthy `hydrated` object was found, leaving the raw id in place otherwise. The plural path deletes `*_ids` regardless of whether any lookups failed, so if `_lookupSubType` returns `null/undefined` for unknown ids, the resulting array will contain `undefined` entries and the original ids are lost. Also, `hydrated || []` is effectively dead since `map` always returns an array, but the more important issue is the unconditional deletion causing unrecoverable/invalid embedded data.

Whether this will "blow up" in a template depends on consumers, but the data-loss/undefined-entry problem is directly introduced by this diff and is verifiable from the code.
